WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.132 --> 00:00:08.380
Families can bring out the best in us, and they can bring out the worst, as we see in the story of King Lear.

2
00:00:08.381 --> 00:00:16.694
The proud, old king does not realize his actions will cause loyalty and love to battle greed and hate—with tragic consequences.

3
00:00:16.694 --> 00:00:20.313
The story begins in the great hall of his castle.

4
00:00:20.313 --> 00:00:25.304
Lear, king of England, had grown tired of his responsibilities.

5
00:00:25.306 --> 00:00:34.447
"It is time," he announced, "to pass on my duties to those who are younger, while I, free of my burden, crawl toward death.

6
00:00:34.448 --> 00:00:39.995
I shall divide my kingdom into three parts, one for each of my daughters."

7
00:00:39.996 --> 00:00:48.446
Lear then unrolled a map. "I will give the biggest part," he said, "to the one who convinces me she loves me most."

8
00:00:48.447 --> 00:00:57.692
He turned to his oldest daughter, Goneril, who stood beside her husband, the Duke of Albany. "Speak first," Lear commanded.

9
00:00:57.694 --> 00:01:08.256
Goneril said, "I love you more than words can say, Father, beyond anything that can be measured, and as much as freedom and life itself."

10
00:01:08.257 --> 00:01:13.884
Smiling insincerely, she went on with more pretty phrases.

11
00:01:13.885 --> 00:01:22.558
Cordelia, Lear's youngest daughter, listened with disgust. She knew her sister would say anything to get as much land as possible.

12
00:01:22.559 --> 00:01:33.225
But Lear was very pleased by his oldest daughter's words. He pointed to a section on the map. "From here to here, all this will be yours and Albany's.

13
00:01:33.227 --> 00:01:39.821
Now, what says my second daughter, Regan, dear wife of the Duke of Cornwall?"

14
00:01:39.822 --> 00:01:48.377
Regan boldly stepped forward. "My sister's words also describe my love for you, except she hasn't gone far enough.

15
00:01:48.378 --> 00:01:54.710
Nothing in life gives me joy except my love for you!" she cried dramatically.

16
00:01:54.711 --> 00:02:00.281
Cordelia was now very upset. "How can I compete with these lies?" she thought.

17
00:02:00.282 --> 00:02:05.448
"Yet, I know my love for my father is greater than I can express."

18
00:02:05.449 --> 00:02:11.118
But again, Lear was delighted, and indicated another part of the map.

19
00:02:11.119 --> 00:02:17.414
"This is for you and Cornwall," he said to Regan, "and it is equal to Goneril's share."

20
00:02:17.415 --> 00:02:28.648
Then he turned to Cordelia. "And you, my youngest and my joy, what can you say to win the remaining third, the finest part of my kingdom? Speak!"

21
00:02:28.649 --> 00:02:32.288
"Nothing, my lord," replied Cordelia quietly.

22
00:02:32.289 --> 00:02:38.506
"Nothing?" said Lear, shocked. "Nothing will come of nothing. Speak again."

23
00:02:38.507 --> 00:02:44.240
Cordelia looked down. She did not want to use flattery, as her sisters had done.

24
00:02:44.241 --> 00:02:50.804
"Dear Father," she said, "I love you as a child should—no more, no less."

25
00:02:50.805 --> 00:02:56.859
Lear was angry now. "Explain yourself, or you may live to regret it!"

26
00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:03.137
"Sir, I obey, love, and honor you as a daughter should," answered Cordelia.

27
00:03:03.139 --> 00:03:07.614
"Why do my sisters have husbands if they say they love you only?

28
00:03:07.615 --> 00:03:12.529
If I marry, it's only fair that my husband take half my love with him."

29
00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:17.170
"So young and so hard-hearted?" said her father, stunned.

30
00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.931
"So young and true, my lord," replied Cordelia.

31
00:03:20.932 --> 00:03:26.685
"Then let truth be your dowry," raged Lear. "You are no longer my daughter.

32
00:03:26.687 --> 00:03:32.535
I looked forward to your care and affection in my old age, but now I disown you!"

33
00:03:32.536 --> 00:03:37.277
Cordelia stared at her father, devastated by his words.

34
00:03:37.278 --> 00:03:43.535
"Your share of the kingdom," the king went on, "will be divided between your sisters and their husbands."

35
00:03:43.536 --> 00:03:49.204
Lear took off his crown and motioned to Albany and Cornwall to step forward.

36
00:03:49.205 --> 00:03:54.899
"Take this, for you are now my sons and will rule the land together."

37
00:03:54.900 --> 00:04:04.758
To Goneril and Regan, he said, "All that is mine is now yours, except for one hundred knights, whom I shall keep to attend to me.

38
00:04:04.759 --> 00:04:09.123
And I shall come live with each of you in turn for one month at a time."

39
00:04:09.125 --> 00:04:18.100
At that moment, the Earl of Kent, Lear's most loyal nobleman, stepped forward. "My lord," he said, "you're behaving rashly.

40
00:04:18.102 --> 00:04:24.521
Your youngest daughter certainly does not love you least. I beg you, reconsider your decision."

41
00:04:24.523 --> 00:04:28.657
"Do not come between the dragon and his wrath!" cried Lear.

42
00:04:28.659 --> 00:04:34.539
"How dare you? I have spoken, and yet, you want me to go back on my word?

43
00:04:34.541 --> 00:04:41.222
You are banished, Kent! If you are found in England ten days from now, you will be put to death!"

44
00:04:41.224 --> 00:04:51.114
Kent bowed his head. "Then fare thee well, my lord." To Cordelia, he said, "May the gods look after you—you spoke fairly and truthfully."

45
00:04:51.129 --> 00:04:53.296
He turned to Goneril and Regan.

46
00:04:53.298 --> 00:04:56.749
"And I hope your deeds will match your fancy speeches."

47
00:04:56.749 --> 00:05:00.535
Bidding everyone good-bye, Kent left the castle.

48
00:05:00.535 --> 00:05:03.551
There was one more matter to be resolved that day.

49
00:05:03.552 --> 00:05:13.190
Cordelia, the only daughter who wasn't married, had two suitors at court: the Duke of Burgundy and the king of France. Lear called them in.

50
00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:19.911
"Lord Burgundy," he said, "do you still want my daughter? She has lost my love and her dowry."

51
00:05:19.913 --> 00:05:24.166
"Give her back the lands you promised her, and I'll marry her," replied the duke.

52
00:05:24.168 --> 00:05:26.881
But Lear would not change his mind.

53
00:05:26.883 --> 00:05:34.375
"Then I am sorry," said the duke to Cordelia. "Having lost a father, you must also lose a husband."

54
00:05:34.376 --> 00:05:40.351
"What has she done that you would disown your most beloved daughter?" the king of France asked.

55
00:05:40.352 --> 00:05:48.215
Cordelia spoke. "Sir, my offense was simply that I would rather show my love to my father than speak of it."

56
00:05:48.216 --> 00:05:56.485
"Is that all?" said the king of France. He truly loved Cordelia; he did not care that she had lost her third of the kingdom.

57
00:05:56.486 --> 00:06:01.128
"Come with me to France, Cordelia," he said gently, "and be my queen."

58
00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:07.899
Cordelia turned to Goneril and Regan. "You are my sisters, so I do not want to speak badly of you.

59
00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:15.708
But knowing you both the way I do, I fear for my father's welfare. Still, try to love him well."

60
00:06:15.709 --> 00:06:19.748
"Don't tell us our duty," Goneril snapped back.

61
00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:28.344
The king of France then took Cordelia's arm. "Come, dearest. We shall leave for France tonight." And they left the great hall.

62
00:06:28.345 --> 00:06:35.775
Goneril drew Regan aside. "Sister," she whispered, "I think our father will be coming to stay with me tonight.

63
00:06:35.776 --> 00:06:40.598
So you and I need to talk now. You see how strange he's acting?

64
00:06:40.598 --> 00:06:46.739
He always loved our sister the most, and now he's rejected her. And he banished Kent too!

65
00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:50.821
It must be his age. He's going to be nothing but trouble."

66
00:06:50.821 --> 00:06:56.132
"And it's only going to get worse as he grows older," muttered Regan.

67
00:06:56.133 --> 00:07:01.702
"We must do something about him," her sister said. "And we must act fast."

68
00:07:01.703 --> 00:07:11.666
Also present in the great hall was Edmund, a handsome young man who had watched Lear disinherit Cordelia. Her situation reminded him of his own.

69
00:07:11.667 --> 00:07:18.761
Edmund was the younger—and illegitimate—son of the Earl of Gloucester, a trusted friend of Lear.

70
00:07:18.762 --> 00:07:23.377
The earl had an older son, Edgar, who was his legal heir.

71
00:07:23.377 --> 00:07:33.264
"The law says I shall never inherit anything from my father either," thought Edmund. "That is not fair. I'm just as brave and clever as my brother.

72
00:07:33.265 --> 00:07:43.416
In fact, I'm more clever. Because I have a plan to become my father's only son and heir—I shall get rid of Edgar!"

